WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.980
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Stackdriver
improves the observability

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.630
of our services,
and it helps us put

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.630
SRE principles into practice.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:07.380
But how can you try
some of these features

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:10.040
without impacting your
production monitoring setup?

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.790
With the Stackdriver
Sandbox, you

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.380
have a fully-instrumented
environment

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.310
ready to test in minutes.

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.220
I'll show you how to
set it up right now.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.690
This is "The Stack Doctor."

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:22.050
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.540
The Stackdriver Sandbox
is an open source project

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.542
that helps you learn
how to use Stackdriver.

00:00:28.542 --> 00:00:30.500
It includes everything
you need to get started.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.420
It provisions a demo
application composed

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:35.510
of multiple microservices
and configures monitoring.

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:37.590
You can try the project
by visiting its home page

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.850
at stackdriver-sandbox.dev
and the associated GitHub

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:41.930
repository.

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.360
We'll put links to both
in the description below.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.787
I'm here to show you how to
create a sandbox of your own

00:00:46.787 --> 00:00:48.620
and use it to try
Stackdriver features we've

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:50.300
covered in previous episodes.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.070
Start by going to
the Sandbox homepage.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.040
From here, simply click on
the Open in Cloud Shell button

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.980
to open Cloud Shell and
download the project repository.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.140
Run the install script
in the current directory.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:02.390
The script will take a few
minutes to build and deploy

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:03.500
all of the components.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.870
While it's running, let's
review what's happening.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:08.420
The script is creating a
new Google Cloud project.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:10.730
Within that project, it's
creating a Kubernetes Engine

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:13.550
cluster and deploying the
sample application to it.

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:16.280
Finally, it's creating a
Compute Engine virtual machine

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.135
that'll be used to generate
load against your application.

00:01:19.135 --> 00:01:20.510
When the script
finishes running,

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.880
it provides you with
four URLs to access.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.400
The first takes you to
the Stackdriver interface

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.027
where you go through the process
of setting up your workspace.

00:01:28.027 --> 00:01:30.110
You'll need to confirm the
creation of a workspace

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.640
and add any other
projects you may

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.480
have to be monitored as well.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.140
Once the setup is done, you'll
be ready to use Stackdriver.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.650
The second URL takes you to
the Cloud Console for you

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:40.850
in your project,
and the third URL

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.100
takes you to the home page
for your sample application

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.510
running in Kubernetes Engine.

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:47.420
The fourth URL takes you to
the load generation tool.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.330
Use it to simulate 20 users
with a hatch rate of two, which

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.760
means that two users will
be added every few seconds

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.680
until we get up to the full 20.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.450
Now that your application
is under load,

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.485
you can use Stackdriver
to observe it.

00:01:58.485 --> 00:02:00.110
Start by viewing the
Kubernetes cluster

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:02.360
by selecting Kubernetes
Engine from the Resources menu

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:03.625
in the Cloud Console.

00:02:03.625 --> 00:02:05.000
You can use this
dashboard to see

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.083
the performance of your
infrastructure, workloads,

00:02:07.083 --> 00:02:10.009
and services, and you can click
on each row in the dashboard

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:12.630
to get metrics and
logs for that resource.

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:14.780
Because this is a distributed
application composed

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.450
of multiple microservices,
understanding service

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:18.920
latency is key.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.500
Click on Trace on the left side
and then go to the trace list

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.420
to see the request being traced.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.550
Then click on a request
to see how it traverses

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:28.850
the services in the application
and latency for each service

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:29.960
call.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.180
Finally, to understand the
performance and resource

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:35.540
utilization of your services
themselves, go to a Profiler.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:37.730
Select the product catalog
service and CPU time

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:38.990
as the metric.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:41.810
Profiler shows CPU utilization
down to the method level,

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:44.180
and you can use this data
to optimize both service

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.242
performance and
resource utilization.

00:02:46.242 --> 00:02:48.200
We've built the Stackdriver
Sandbox to give you

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.810
an opportunity to try these
and other Stackdriver features

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:52.323
quickly and easily.

00:02:52.323 --> 00:02:53.990
We'll come back to
it in future episodes

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:56.282
and use it to learn about
features like Error Reporting

00:02:56.282 --> 00:02:56.900
and Debugger.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.400
Thank you for joining me today,
and come back soon for more.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:00.530
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:01.550
Stay healthy out there.

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:04.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

